{
  // Typecheck project for the browser-context sources ONLY: `engine/test-browser/**`,
  // which runs inside a page (and a real `Worker`) under
  // `engine/vitest.browser.config.ts`.
  //
  // ## Why this is a SEPARATE project rather than `lib: ["DOM"]` in the base
  //
  // `engine/package.json` describes the engine as "pure TypeScript, zero DOM"; that
  // is a portability boundary, not a slogan — the same `engine/src` has to compile
  // and run under Node, a Web Worker and (per `vitest.browser.config.ts`) three
  // different JS engines. `tsconfig.base.json` therefore sets `lib: ["ES2022"]` with
  // no DOM, so any accidental `document`/`window`/`Worker` reference inside
  // `engine/src` is a compile error rather than a runtime surprise on the platform
  // that lacks it.
  //
  // `engine/tsconfig.json` used to include `test-browser/**` in that DOM-free
  // program. That could never have typechecked — the browser tests legitimately
  // construct `new Worker(...)`, set `MessagePort.onmessage` and read
  // `MessageEvent<T>.data` — and it did not: it produced 7 errors, which is why
  // `npm run typecheck` (and therefore all of `npm run ci`) exited 2 at its first
  // step. Adding DOM to the base would have silenced them by DELETING the boundary
  // for every file in the tree, which is the opposite of the fix.
  //
  // Splitting the browser sources into their own project gives them exactly the
  // globals their host has, and leaves `engine/tsconfig.json` — the program that
  // covers `engine/src/**` and the Node tests — DOM-free. `engine/src` is compiled
  // by BOTH projects (the browser tests import it), so the boundary is still
  // enforced: a DOM reference added to `engine/src` stays an error in
  // `engine/tsconfig.json`, and being permitted by this one changes nothing about
  // that. `engine/test/no-dom.boundary.test.ts` asserts the arrangement so it cannot
  // silently regress.
  //
  // Wired into CI via engine's `typecheck` script, which runs both projects.
  "extends": "../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ES2022 stays; DOM + DOM.Iterable are what a page and a Worker actually have.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    // No `["node"]`. @types/node redeclares `MessagePort`, `MessageEvent`, `Worker`
    // and `performance` with worker_threads/undici shapes that do NOT match the web
    // ones, so leaving it on would keep resolving `onmessage` and `MessageEvent<T>`
    // against the Node declarations and reproduce the very errors this project
    // exists to fix. Nothing under `test-browser/` imports a `node:` module — by
    // construction, since it runs in a browser.
    "types": []
  },
  "include": ["test-browser/**/*.ts"]
}
